;;; P1.5 demo 6 — one-step lookahead (S1.5.6): ENABLED.
;;;
;;; The ENABLED half of an on/off pair. 06_lookahead_on.ein and
;;; 07_lookahead_off.ein are byte-identical except for the single
;;; `(config …)` line below — diff the two files to see it.
;;;
;;; The puzzle: five colours, five houses, four colours anchored.
;;; Blue is the lone unanchored colour and must take the one free
;;; house, H5. The hypothesis generator emits `(co-located Blue Hk)`
;;; for k = 1..5. Four of them are *doomed*: placing Blue in an
;;; already-occupied house Hk chains, in ONE rule firing against the
;;; already-saturated root KB,
;;;
;;;     (co-located Blue Hk)        -- the hypothesis
;;;   + (co-located Hk Ck)          -- symmetric of the Ck↔Hk anchor
;;;   ⇒ transitive ⇒ (co-located Blue Ck)
;;;
;;; and `(not (co-located Blue Ck))` was already derived by
;;; `sibling-exclusive` (Blue and Ck are both Colours). The lookahead
;;; sees that collision and drops the candidate.
;;;
;;; With the lookahead ON (this file) those four candidates never
;;; fork — the search tree collapses to the lone surviving placement.
;;; With it OFF (07_lookahead_off.ein) each doomed colour forks,
;;; saturates, and dies as its own branch, so the tree is an order of
;;; magnitude larger.
;;;
;;; ~~Same verdict (Solution) and the same binding (?h = H5) either
;;; way — only the node count differs. Measured: ON (06) = 2 tree
;;; nodes; OFF (07) = 82 (80 dead branches).~~ **That was P1.5, and it
;;; is no longer true** (re-measured 2026-08-20, M1a S1a.6.7, with
;;; `ein solve <file>` and `ein solve <file> -e`):
;;;
;;;     ON  (06)  fast: Solution  k=1   67 enterings
;;;               -e:   Ambiguity k=22  5173 enterings
;;;     OFF (07)  fast: Contradiction k=0  11501 enterings
;;;               -e:   Contradiction k=0  11501 enterings
;;;
;;; The lever changes the VERDICT, not just the node count, because
;;; `hypgen::complete` asks the hypothesis
;;; generator whether anything is undecided — and the generator's
;;; candidate list is itself lookahead-filtered. A doomed candidate is
;;; *decided* with the lookahead on and *open* with it off, so a KB
;;; whose only survivors are doomed is a solution node in the first
;;; case and no model at all in the second. See
;;; docs/kernel/inference/features.md and plans/followups/
;;; f4_cross_cutting.md Q40.

;; Its `(config :enable-pre-branch-lookahead true)` line went at M1e2 S1e2.1c.5 (2026-11-03),
;; when the user retired the flag: no route reads it, and a program that sets
;; it is refused at load.
;;
;; **No lever changes the answer** (the user's ruling of 2026-10-15,
;; docs/kernel/configuration.md § 2.5), so the verdicts measured above for the
;; two halves were the walk's, and the OFF half's `Contradiction` was wrong:
;; register row W1/branching-07, confirmed at the revisit (M1e2 S1e2.1.7,
;; 2026-11-03). Since the line above went, 06 and 07 are one program, and
;; their answer is R: 22 maximal states of k_s = 74, which `ein solve
;; --maximal` reports. That every one of them places a colour in a *type*
;; (`Color`, `House`) is C1's question, Q-M1e.12.
;;
;; **Not wrong** (register row C1/branching-06, the user's ruling of
;; 2026-11-03, Q-M1e.12 (a)): this program is under-specified.
;; `(relation co-located T T)` admits `(co-located Blue Color)`, and the engine
;; does what the signature says. M1e2 S1e2.2.10 retypes the relation here and
;; in 07, as `04_two_levels.ein`'s S1.7.23 signature does, and warns when an
;; untyped rung binds a type as an object.
;;
;; **Retyped 2026-11-04 by M1e2 S1e2.2.10**, on that ruling: `co-located`
;; relates a Color to a House, `(relation co-located Color House)`, as
;; 04_two_levels.ein's signature does, where it was `(relation co-located T
;; T)`. A name a relation signature declares is no candidate object, so the
;; blind enumerator guesses over the ten leaves alone: H is 132 candidates
;; (42 of co-located, 90 of is-a), 130 of them dead alone, and R is two
;; states over four commitments, ∅ and the state three commitments reach,
;; {(co-located Blue H5)} with its mirror: Blue in the one free house, ?h =
;; H5, as derived above. `ein solve --maximal` lists that one state, and
;; `--single` says Ambiguity because ∅ is in R too. No `types` row: nothing H
;; proposes names a type.
;;
;; Every count above this section is the untyped program's, the file at
;; 86df5456 and before: H was 204 candidates, 72 of them naming Color or
;; House, R 74 states, and its maximal line 22, each binding a colour to a
;; type. On that shape every read-out now prints a `types` row under
;; `universe` (`ein solve examples/branching/02_one_dead_one_alive.ein` is
;; one): the names on the right of an is-a that the blind enumerator, which
;; is untyped, binds as objects because no signature names them.

(rule symmetric (?rel)
  :match  (?rel ?a ?b)
  :assert (?rel ?b ?a)
  :why    "{?rel} symmetric")

(rule transitive (?rel)
  :match  (and (?rel ?a ?b) (?rel ?b ?c) (neq ?a ?c))
  :assert (?rel ?a ?c)
  :why    "{?rel} transitive")

(rule sibling-exclusive (?siblings-via ?exclusive-under)
  :match  (and (?siblings-via ?a ?T) (?siblings-via ?b ?T) (neq ?a ?b))
  :assert (not (?exclusive-under ?a ?b))
  :why    "{?a} and {?b} share {?T} — exclusive under {?exclusive-under}.")

(rule functional (?R)
  :match  (and (?R ?a ?b) (?R ?a ?c) (neq ?b ?c))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?R} not functional")

(relation is-a       T T)
(relation co-located Color House)

(symmetric         co-located)
(transitive        co-located)
(sibling-exclusive is-a co-located)
(functional        is-a)

(is-a Color T) (is-a House T)
(is-a Red    Color) (is-a Green Color) (is-a Ivory  Color)
(is-a Yellow Color) (is-a Blue  Color)
(is-a H1 House) (is-a H2 House) (is-a H3 House)
(is-a H4 House) (is-a H5 House)

(co-located Red    H1 :source "(1)")
(co-located Green  H2 :source "(2)")
(co-located Ivory  H3 :source "(3)")
(co-located Yellow H4 :source "(4)")

(query
  :goal (co-located Blue ?h))
